\fs96 \cf2 Mojo changelog\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 This is a list of changes to the Mojo language, standard library, and tools.\
To check your current version, run 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 mojo --version
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . To install or update Mojo, see the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/install/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 install guide}}.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 v0.26.2 (2026-03-19)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs48 \cf2 \uc0\u10024 
\f0  Highlights\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0
\fs32 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Official Modular AI agent skills. Modular has released {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://github.com/modular/skills"}}{\fldrslt \ul official AI agent skills}} for working with MAX and Mojo. The four skills\'97
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 new-modular-project
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 mojo-syntax
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 mojo-gpu-fundamentals
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 mojo-python-interop
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 \'97help AI coding agents create new projects with the right tooling, generate correct modern Mojo syntax, follow GPU programming best practices, and handle Python-Mojo interop. The skills follow the open {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://agentskills.io/specification"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 Agent Skills Standard}} and work with Claude Code and other compatible agents. Install them with 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 npx skills add modular/skills
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Conditional trait conformances. Structs can now declare trait conformances that apply only when type parameters satisfy certain conditions, using 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 where
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  clauses in the conformance list. Nine standard library types\'97including 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 List
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Dict
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Set
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Optional
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 \'97now use this feature to enforce trait requirements at compile time. See \cf5 \ul \ulc5 Language enhancements\cf2 \ulnone  and \cf5 \ul Conditional conformances\cf2 \ulnone .\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
def
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 /
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 fn
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  unification. 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 def
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is now Mojo's standard function declaration keyword. 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 def
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  functions no longer implicitly raise and now have the same semantics as 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 fn
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 : non-raising by default, with explicit 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 raises
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  for functions that can throw. 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 fn
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is deprecated and will be removed in a future release. See \cf5 \ul Language enhancements\cf2 \ulnone .\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
T-strings. Mojo now supports template strings with the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 t"..."
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  prefix. T-strings produce a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 TString
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value that captures both the static format string and runtime arguments, enabling structured string processing without immediate allocation. See \cf5 \ul Language enhancements\cf2 \ulnone .\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
comptime if
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime for
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . The new 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime if
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime for
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  syntax replaces the legacy 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 @parameter if
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 @parameter for
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  decorator forms for compile-time conditionals and loops. Both syntaxes are accepted in this release; the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 @parameter
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  forms will be deprecated soon. See \cf5 \ul Language enhancements\cf2 \ulnone .\
\ls4\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
assert
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  statement. Mojo now supports a standalone 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 assert
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  statement, similar to Python's 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 assert
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . It checks a condition at runtime and aborts if the condition is false, with an optional message. Under the hood, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 assert
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  desugars to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 debug_assert()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and respects the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 -D ASSERT
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  flag. See \cf5 \ul Language enhancements\cf2 \ulnone .\
\ls4\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
@align(N)
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  decorator. Structs can now specify minimum alignment with 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 @align(N)
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , similar to C++ 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 alignas
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and Rust 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 #[repr(align(N))]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . The alignment value can be a struct parameter, enabling generic cache-aligned and hardware-aligned types. See \cf5 \ul Language enhancements\cf2 \ulnone .\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Implicit 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 SIMD
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  conversions deprecated. Implicit conversions from 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 SIMD
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  scalar types like 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int8
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  or 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Float32
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  are now deprecated. Code relying on these conversions should use explicit constructors instead. The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 mojo build --experimental-fixit
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  command can assist with migration. See \cf5 \ul Math and numeric types\cf2 \ulnone .\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Init unification. The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __moveinit__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __copyinit__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  methods are now renamed to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __init__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  with keyword-only 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 take
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 copy
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  arguments, respectively. The legacy names are still accepted but should be migrated to the modern form. See \cf5 \ul Init unification\cf2 \ulnone .\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Stringable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Representable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  deprecated. These traits are replaced by the unified 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Writable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  trait, which provides default implementations. Most standard library types have already had their 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Stringable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Representable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  conformances removed. See \cf5 \ul String and text\cf2 \ulnone .\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
UTF-8 safety improvements. 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 StringSlice
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  subscripting now panics when an index falls in the middle of a multi-byte codepoint, and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String.resize()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  panics on operations that would produce invalid UTF-8. Byte-position subscripting now returns an entire Unicode codepoint instead of a single byte. See \cf5 \ul String and text\cf2 \ulnone .\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
trait
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  no longer inherits 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ImplicitlyDestructible
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . Trait declarations no longer automatically conform to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ImplicitlyDestructible
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ; traits that need it must now opt in explicitly. This continues v0.26.1's work on explicitly-destroyed types and encourages the ecosystem to support linear types by default. See \cf5 \ul Other changes\cf2 \ulnone .\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Documentation\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0
\fs32 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Added a new {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/mojo/manual/python-to-mojo/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul Mojo tips for Python devs}} page to the Mojo Manual, covering key differences Python developers encounter when learning Mojo. Topics include variable declarations with 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 var
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , type annotations, integer vs. floating-point division, implicit conversions, and other common gotchas. If you're coming from Python, this page helps you avoid surprises and write idiomatic Mojo faster.\
\ls5\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Added a new {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/lifecycle/initialization/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul Instance initialization deep dive}} page to the Mojo Manual. This page explains the difference between logical and fieldwise initialization, how to write 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __init__
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  constructors that satisfy the compiler's field-by-field initialization requirements, and how copy and move constructors fit into the initialization model. Essential reading for anyone working with non-trivial struct types.\
\ls5\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Added a new {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/generics/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul Generics}} page to the Mojo Manual with worked examples of conditional trait conformance. The page shows how to use 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 where
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  clauses to declare that a generic type conforms to a trait only when its type parameters meet certain conditions, such as making a container 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Equatable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  only when its element type is.\
\ls5\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Restructured and expanded the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/metaprogramming/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul metaprogramming}} section of the Mojo Manual. This includes a new introduction page, which provides an overview of Mojo's compile-time metaprogramming model, and new pages on compile-time evaluation, reflection, and materialization, as well as the previously-mentioned page on generics. Together these changes cover several previously undocumented topics and provide a smoother learning path from basic parameters to advanced metaprogramming techniques.\
\ls5\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Updated the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/tools/notebooks/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul Jupyter Notebooks}} page with Apple Silicon GPU examples and setup instructions, so macOS users with M-series chips can run GPU-accelerated Mojo code in notebooks without needing an external GPU.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Language enhancements\
