\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 from pkg import *
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  now correctly resolve to the function rather than the module.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls10\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
is_compile_time()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  has been renamed to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __is_run_in_comptime_interpreter
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , which is now a keyword rather than a function call. It provides a mechanism for supporting different code execution paths in the comptime interpreter versus runtime-generated code. This value cannot be used as a constant in any comptime expressions because it is always evaluated as 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 True
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  for comptime expressions. Use the check as a condition for runtime 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 if
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . The compiler now warns if it is used as a condition for 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime if
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls10\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Mojo supports indexing with subscripts and names using the standard Python 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __getitem__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __getattr__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  methods. Previously it used a heuristic to determine whether a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __get*__
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method was operated with dynamic or parameter indexes. Mojo now has a simple and explicit policy: if a type implements a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __getitem_param__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  or 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __getattr_param__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method and the indices are valid parameter expressions, the compiler will pick it (but will not support a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __setitem__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  pair). If not or if the indices are only valid runtime values, Mojo will try 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __getitem__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 /
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __setitem__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  as usual. This makes the behavior more predictable and explicit, but requires types to switch to the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 param
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method names if they desire parameter-style subscripting. This only affects a small number of special types like 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Tuple
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 VariadicPack
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
\ls10\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 @doc_private
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  decorator has been renamed to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 @doc_hidden
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to better reflect its purpose of hiding declarations from documentation generation, rather than implying any change in access control. The old 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 @doc_private
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  name is still accepted but deprecated and will be removed in a future release.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

