\fs40 \cf2 Testing\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls19\ilvl0
\fs32 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/testing/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul testing}} module now provides {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/testing/testing/assert_equal/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul assert_equal}} and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/testing/testing/assert_not_equal/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul assert_not_equal}} overloads for 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Tuple
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , enabling direct tuple-to-tuple comparisons in tests instead of element-by-element assertions. Element types must conform to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Equatable & Writable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls19\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
assert_equal
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 assert_not_equal
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  now work with types implementing 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Writable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs40 \cf2 Other library changes\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls20\ilvl0
\fs32 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Changed {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/sys/info/CompilationTarget/#unsupported_target_error"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul CompilationTarget.unsupported_target_error()}} to return 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Never
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and removed its 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 result
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type parameter.\
\ls20\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Removed 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DType.get_dtype[T]()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DType.is_scalar[T]()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . These were low-level operations for extracting the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DType
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  of a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 SIMD
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  in generic code. There are better alternatives available in Mojo today using reflection capabilities.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls20\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/time/time/perf_counter_ns/"}}{\fldrslt \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\ul perf_counter_ns()}}
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
 now returns correct nanoseconds on GPU instead of raw cycle counts. Previously on NVIDIA GPUs it used 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 clock64
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  (a cycle counter dependent on GPU core clock frequency); it now uses 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 globaltimer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  which provides actual nanosecond resolution. On AMD GPUs, it now uses 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 s_memrealtime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  (a constant-speed real-time clock) instead of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 s_memtime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  (a cycle counter).\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls20\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/mojo/kernels/buffer/dimlist/DimList/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul DimList}} type has moved to representing its dimensions as parameters to the type instead of values inside the type, directly reflecting that the dimensions are known at compile time. Change 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DimList(x, y)
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DimList[x, y]()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Tooling changes\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls21\ilvl0
\fs32 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The Mojo compiler now accepts conjoined 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 -D
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  options in addition to the non-conjoined form as before. Now, both 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 -Dfoo
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 -D foo
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  are accepted.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls21\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
mojo build
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  now supports several 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 --print-*
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  options for discovering target configuration and supported architectures:\
\pard\tx940\tx1440\pardeftab720\li1440\fi-1440\partightenfactor0
\ls21\ilvl1
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	
\f3 \uc0\u9702 
\f0 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
--print-effective-target
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 : Shows the resolved target configuration after processing all command-line flags.\
\ls21\ilvl1
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	
\f3 \uc0\u9702 
\f0 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
--print-supported-targets
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 : Lists all available LLVM target architectures.\
\ls21\ilvl1
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	
\f3 \uc0\u9702 
\f0 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
--print-supported-cpus
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 : Lists valid CPU names for a given target triple (requires 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 --target-triple
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ).\
\ls21\ilvl1
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	
\f3 \uc0\u9702 
\f0 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
--print-supported-accelerators
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 : Lists all supported GPU and accelerator architectures (NVIDIA, AMD, Apple Metal).\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls21\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
mojo format
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  now only formats Mojo files (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 .mojo
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 .
\f2 \uc0\u55357 \u56613 
\f0\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ) by default when run on a directory. Previously it would also format Python files, which conflicted with Python-specific formatters in pre-commit hooks. Users who want to format Python files can use 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 mblack
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  directly.\
\ls21\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
mojo format
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  now supports 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 --print-cache-dir
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  (hidden, use 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 --help-hidden
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to see it) to display the path to the formatter cache directory.\
\ls21\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
mojo build --emit=asm
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  now also emits GPU kernel assembly files alongside the host 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 .s
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  output. For NVIDIA targets, PTX is written as 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 <out>_<kernelfn>.ptx
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ; for Apple Metal targets, LLVM IR is written as 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 <out>_<kernelfn>.ll
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ; for AMD targets, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 .amdgcn
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  files are written. Multiple kernels generated from the same function are disambiguated with a numeric suffix (for example, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 <out>_<kernelfn>_1.ptx
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ).\
\ls21\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
mojo doc
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is now more consistent with Python in its treatment of private members. Any member name starting with an underscore (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 _
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ) is treated as private unless it is a "dunder" member (starting and ending with double-underscores). In practice this means that members that start but don't end with double underscores (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __example()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ) are now treated as private. Dunder methods that start with 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __mlir
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  are now treated as public unless marked with the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 @doc_private
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  decorator.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs48 \cf2 \uc0\u10060 
\f0  Removed\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls22\ilvl0
\fs32 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 .
\f2 \uc0\u55357 \u56613 
\f0\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  (flame) and 
\f2\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \uc0\u55357 \u56550 
