\f0  Highlights\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls24\ilvl0
\fs32 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Expanded reflection module. The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 reflection
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  module now provides extensive compile-time introspection: struct field enumeration, types, and byte offsets; source location tracking; and trait conformance checking on dynamically obtained types. These APIs enable advanced metaprogramming patterns like automatic serialization and debug formatting. See \cf5 \ul Reflection and introspection\cf2 \ulnone .\
\ls24\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Explicitly-destroyed types. Mojo now has first-class support for explicitly-destroyed types (sometimes referred to as "linear types"). The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 AnyType
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Movable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Copyable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  traits no longer require a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __del__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method; use 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ImplicitlyDestructible
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  when you need implicit destruction. Explicitly-destroyed types let you encode powerful invariants requiring explicit resource handling. See \cf5 \ul Language changes\cf2 \ulnone  and \cf5 \ul Explicitly-destroyed types\cf2 \ulnone .\
\ls24\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Typed errors. Functions can now specify what type they raise instead of defaulting to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Error
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  (for example, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 fn foo() raises CustomError -> Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ). Typed errors are highly efficient\'97they compile to an alternate return value with no stack unwinding\'97making them suitable for GPU and embedded targets. See \cf5 \ul Language enhancements\cf2 \ulnone .\
\ls24\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Traits with default implementations. The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Hashable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Writable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Equatable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  traits now provide default implementations that automatically derive behavior from struct fields using reflection. Simple structs can conform to these traits without writing any boilerplate\'97just ensure all fields conform to the same trait. See \cf5 \ul Traits with default implementations\cf2 \ulnone .\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls24\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
UInt
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type redesign. The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 UInt
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  struct has been replaced by a type alias to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Scalar[DType.uint]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , enabling more powerful generic programming over unsigned SIMD data types of machine word size. See \cf5 \ul Other library changes\cf2 \ulnone .\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls24\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
String UTF-8 safety. 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  now offers three explicit constructors for raw bytes: 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 from_utf8=
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  (validates and raises on error), 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 from_utf8_lossy=
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  (replaces invalid sequences with ), and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 unsafe_from_utf8=
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  (no validation). This makes UTF-8 handling guarantees explicit at construction time. See \cf5 \ul String and text\cf2 \ulnone .\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

