\fs48 \cf2 Language enhancements\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls26\ilvl0
\fs32 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Mojo now supports raising "typed errors", where a function can specify what type it raises instead of defaulting to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/error/Error/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul Error}} type. This is done by specifying it after the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 raises
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  keyword, for example, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 fn foo() raises CustomError -> Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\uc0\u8232 Raised errors in Mojo are very efficient - they work as an alternate return value: for example, a function like 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 fn () raises Int -> Float32:
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  compiles into code that returns either an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  or a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Float32
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and uses an implicit boolean result to determine which one is valid - there is no expensive stack unwinding or slow dynamic logic that is implied. This means that thrown errors work fine on GPUs and other embedded targets.\uc0\u8232 The 'caught' type in a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 try
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  block is automatically inferred to be the first thrown type inside of the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 try
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  body, for example:\uc0\u8232 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 try:\cf14 \cb1 \
