\fs48 \cf2 Language changes\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls27\ilvl0
\fs32 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The Mojo language basic trait hierarchy has changed to expand first-class support for explicitly-destroyed types (sometimes referred to as "linear types").\uc0\u8232 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/anytype/AnyType/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul AnyType}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/value/Movable/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul Movable}}, and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/value/Copyable/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul Copyable}} traits no longer require that a type provide a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __del__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method that may be called by the compiler implicitly whenever an owned value is unused. Instead, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/anytype/ImplicitlyDestructible/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul ImplicitlyDestructible}} trait should be used in generic code to require that a type is implicitly destructible.\uc0\u8232 Explicitly-destroyed types enable Mojo programs to encode powerful invariants in the type system, by modeling a type in such a way that a user is required to take an action "in the future", rather than simply implicitly dropping an instance "on the floor".\u8232 Code using 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 T: AnyType
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  can change to use 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 T: ImplicitlyDestructible
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to preserve its pre-existing behavior following this change.\uc0\u8232 Relatedly, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/anytype/#unknowndestructibility"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul UnknownDestructibility}} trait is now no longer required, as it is equivalent to the new 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 AnyType
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  behavior.\
\ls27\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Mojo no longer supports overloading functions on parameters alone: it will not try to disambiguate between 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 fn foo[a: Int8]():
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 fn foo[a: Int32]():
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  for example. Mojo never fully implemented the previous support in a reliable way, and removing this simplifies the language. It still supports overloading on function arguments of course.\
\ls27\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __next_ref__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method in for-each loops has been removed. Now you can implement the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __next__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method of your iterator to return either a value or a reference. When directly using the collection, Mojo will use the ref-returning variant, but will allow it to conform to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/iter/Iterator/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul Iterator}} for use with generic algorithms (which use a copied value).\
\ls27\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 origin_of(x)
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  operator now returns a value of type 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Origin
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  instead of an internal MLIR type, and aliases like 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ImmutOrigin
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  are now 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Origin
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type as well.\
\ls27\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Origin.cast_from[x]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  syntax has been replaced with a safe implicit conversion from any origin to an immutable origin (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ImmutOrigin(x)
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ) and an explicit unsafe conversion (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 unsafe_origin_mutcast[origin, mut=m]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ).\
\ls27\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 *_
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 **_
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  syntax for explicitly unpacked parameters has been replaced with a simplified 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ...
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  syntax. Instead of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 T[4, 5, *_, **_]
