Basic support for explicitly-destroyed types in the standard library is now available. Explicitly-destroyed types\'97sometimes referred to as "linear types"\'97are types that don't define a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __del__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method that the compiler can call automatically to destroy an instance. Instead, a explicitly-destroyed type must provide a named method taking 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 deinit self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  that the programmer is required to call explicitly whenever an owned instance is no longer used.\uc0\u8232 The updated 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 AnyType
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  trait can be used in parameters to denote generic code that supports object instances that can't be implicitly destroyed.\
\pard\tx940\tx1440\pardeftab720\li1440\fi-1440\partightenfactor0
