\fs64 \cf2 v25.4 (2025-06-18)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs48 \cf2 \uc0\u10024 
\f0  Highlights\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls82\ilvl0
\fs32 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Mojo now supports AMD GPUs, expanding hardware compatibility beyond NVIDIA to include AMD's GPU ecosystem. This enables Mojo applications to leverage AMD's RDNA and CDNA architectures for high-performance computing workloads, providing developers with greater flexibility in choosing hardware platforms for AI and compute-intensive applications.\
\ls82\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Primitives for working with NVIDIA Blackwell GPUs have been added, providing low-level access to the latest GPU architecture features. These primitives enable developers to take advantage of Blackwell's enhanced compute capabilities, improved memory bandwidth, and advanced AI acceleration features, including support for newer tensor operations and optimized memory management patterns.\
\ls82\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The Python-Mojo bindings are available as a preview release! This is the ability to call into Mojo functions from existing Python codebases. The use case is to speed up hot spots/slow Python code by rewriting certain portions of your code in Mojo to achieve performance.\
\ls82\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Mojo collection types received many enhancements.\
\pard\tx940\tx1440\pardeftab720\li1440\fi-1440\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls82\ilvl1
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	
\f3 \uc0\u9702 
\f0 	}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/collections/list/List/"}}{\fldrslt \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\ul List}}
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/collections/set/Set/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul Set}}, and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/collections/dict/Dict/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul Dict}} literals have been reimplemented to provide Python-equivalent features and syntax, including simple literals like 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 [1, 2, 3]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \{k1: v1, k2: v2\}
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
\pard\tx940\tx1440\pardeftab720\li1440\fi-1440\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls82\ilvl1\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	
\f3 \uc0\u9702 
\f0 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
List comprehensions like 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 [a*b for a in range(10) if isprime(a) for b in range(20)]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  as well as dictionary and set comprehensions are now supported.\
\ls82\ilvl1\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	
\f3 \uc0\u9702 
\f0 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Iterating over a collection with a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 for
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  loop no longer requires using the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 []
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  deference operator.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls82\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
See \cf5 \ul Language enhancements\cf2 \ulnone  and \cf5 \ul Standard library changes\cf2 \ulnone  for more details.\
\ls82\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The entire MAX Kernel library is now fully open sourced! For more information, see the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/lib/#max-ai-kernels-library"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul MAX AI kernels library reference}} and the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://github.com/modular/modular/tree/modular/v26.2/max/kernels"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul MAX AI kernels source}}.\
\ls82\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Mojo is now available on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://godbolt.org/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul Godbolt.org}}, which is also known as the "Compiler Explorer".\
