\fs48 \cf2 Language enhancements\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls83\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
var
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  declarations in functions now support more flexible "patterns", allowing multiple values to be declared at once, for example 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 var a, b = 4, 5
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 var a, b : Int, Float64
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls83\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Mojo now supports the use of Python-style type patterns when declaring variables on first assignment without the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 var
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  keyword. For example, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 x = 4; y: UInt8 = 5
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  declares both 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 x
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 y
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , where 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 x
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is inferred to the default type of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  whereas 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 y
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  gets the explicit type 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 UInt8
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . Declaring variables without 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 var
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  gives you a function-scoped name, whereas 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 var
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  makes things scoped to the statement they are in (lexical scoping), such as the body of an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 if
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  statement.\
\ls83\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Mojo now supports 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ref
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  patterns that bind a stored LValue into a named declaration, extending the argument convention into local function scope.\uc0\u8232 This can be useful when you want to do something with a reference, but don't want the conceptual overhead of a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/memory/pointer/Pointer/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul Pointer}}. These are equivalent:\uc0\u8232 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 fn\cf14  \cf15 use_pointer\cf5 (\cf14 your_list\cf5 :\cf14  List\cf5 [\cf14 Int\cf5 ]):\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls83\ilvl0\cf14 \cb13 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
    \cf5 var\cf14  p \cf5 =\cf14  Pointer\cf5 (\cf14 to\cf5 =\cf14 your_list\cf5 [\cf14 i\cf5 ])\cf14   \cf6 # Form a safe pointer\cf14 \cb1 \
\ls83\ilvl0\cb13 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
    \cf5 ...\cf14 \cb1 \
