\fs48 \cf2 Language changes\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls84\ilvl0
\fs32 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Implicit trait conformance is deprecated. Each instance of implicit conformance results in a warning, but compilation still goes through. Soon it will be upgraded into an error. Any code currently relying on implicit conformance should either declare conformances explicitly or, if appropriate, replace empty, non-load-bearing traits with trait compositions.\
\ls84\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Mojo doesn't allow the use of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 out
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  or 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 mut
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  as an argument name any longer. Previously you could use 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 fn x(out: Int)
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , but this causes ambiguity with function types. Please use names like 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 output
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  instead.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls84\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
def
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  arguments are no longer implicitly mutable. If you would like to have a locally mutable argument, declare it 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 owned
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  explicitly.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls84\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Global (file-scope) variables are deprecated. Global variables in Mojo are only partially implemented and are known to cause cryptic errors. Now the Mojo compiler issues a warning on global variable usage.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

