\fs48 \cf2 Language changes\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls91\ilvl0
\fs32 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Mojo can now use {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://github.com/modular/modular/blob/modular/v26.2/mojo/proposals/custom-type-merging.md"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul user-declared 
\fs28 \cb12 __merge_with__()
\fs32 \cb1  dunder methods}} to merge values when using different types in ternary operations. This has been adopted to allow pointers to work naturally with the ternary operator, for example 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 var x = one_pointer if cond else other_pointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
\ls91\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Auto-parameterization now extends to struct metatypes. For example, this declaration 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 fn foo[M: type_of(StringLiteral[_])]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  will auto-parameterize on the unbound parameter of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 StringLiteral
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
\ls91\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The Mojo compiler now warns about stores to values that are never used, e.g.: 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 x = foo(); x = bar()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  will warn about the first assignment to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 x
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  because it is overwritten. You can generally address this by deleting dead code, or by assigning to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 _
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  instead: 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 _ = foo(); x = bar()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . You may also encounter this in variable declarations, e.g. 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 var x = 0; ...; x = foo()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . In this case, change the variable to being declared as uninitialized, e.g. 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 var x: Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . You may also silence this warning entirely for a variable by renaming it to start with an underscore, e.g. 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 _x
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
\ls91\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The Mojo compiler now warns about obsolete use of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 mut self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  in initializers, please switch over to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 fn __init__(out self)
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  instead.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls91\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
def
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  functions now require type annotations on arguments, and treat a missing return type as returning 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 None
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . Previously these defaulted to the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 object
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type which led to a variety of problems. Support for 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 object
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  has been removed until we have time to investigate a proper replacement.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Standard library changes\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 String types in Mojo got several significant improvements:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls92\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/collections/string/string/String/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul String}} type no longer copies data from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/string_literal/StringLiteral/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul StringLiteral}} and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/collections/string/string_slice/#aliases"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul StaticString}} since they are known-static-constant values. This allows us to make construction from these values be implicit, which improves ergonomics and performance together. It also implements the "small string optimization", which avoids heap allocation for common short strings. On a 64-bit system, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  can hold up to 23 bytes inline. Its copy constructor is now O(1), performing string data copy lazily on mutation.\
\ls92\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The types {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/collections/string/string_slice/StringSlice/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul StringSlice}} and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/collections/string/string_slice/#aliases"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul StaticString}} are now part of the prelude, there is no need to import them anymore. These are useful for code that just needs a "view" of string data, not to own and mutate it.\
\ls92\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/string_literal/StringLiteral/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul StringLiteral}} type has been moved to a more reliable "dependent type" design where the value of the string is carried in a parameter instead of a stored member. This defines away a category of compiler crashes when working with 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 StringLiteral
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  that involved attempting to manipulate a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 StringLiteral
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  at run time. As a consequence of this change, many APIs should switch to using {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/collections/string/string_slice/#aliases"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul StaticString}} instead of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 StringLiteral
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . For more information on this "dependent type" design for literals, see the proposal, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://github.com/modular/modular/blob/modular/v26.2/mojo/proposals/fixing-simple-literals.md"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul Fixing Simple Literals in Mojo}}.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls92\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  supports a new 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String(unsafe_uninit_length=x)
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  constructor and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 str.resize(unsafe_uninit_length=x)
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  for clients that want to allocate space that they intend to fill in with custom unsafe initialization patterns. The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String(ptr=x, length=y)
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  constructor has been removed.\
\ls92\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  supports working with legacy C APIs that assume null termination, but the details have changed: 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is now no longer implicitly null-terminated, which means that it is incorrect to assume that 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 str.unsafe_ptr()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  will return a null-terminated string. For that, use the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 str.unsafe_cstr_ptr()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method. It now requires the string to be mutable in order to make null-termination lazy on demand. This improves performance for strings that are not passed to legacy APIs.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls92\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/collections/list/List/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul List}} type has been improved similarly to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to reduce inconsistency and enable power-user features, including removing adding 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 List(unsafe_uninit_length=x)
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 list.resize(unsafe_uninit_size=n)
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  methods avoid initialized memory that the caller plans to overwrite.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls92\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/collections/set/Set/"}}{\fldrslt \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\ul Set}}
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
 now conforms to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/value/Copyable/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul Copyable}} trait so you can store sets in other types of collections (for example, as values in a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Dict
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ).\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls92\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The following traits have been removed in favor of trait composition: 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 EqualityComparableCollectionElement
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 RepresentableCollectionElement
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 TestableCollectionElement
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Testable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 StringableIdentifiable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 StringableCollectionElement
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 IntervalPayload
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 WritableCollectionElement
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ComparableCollectionElement
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 BoolableCollectionElement
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 EqualityComparableWritableCollectionElement
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 EqualityComparableWritableCollectionElementNew
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 CollectionElementNew
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 WritableCollectionElementNew
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\uc0\u8232 For example, you can replace 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 EqualityComparableCollectionElement
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  with 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 EqualityComparable & CollectionElement
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 StringableCollectionElement
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  was already deprecated and scheduled to be removed; it can be replaced with 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Writable & CollectionElement
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
\ls92\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/python/python_object/PythonObject/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul PythonObject}} type is being reworked in preparation for some improvements to Mojo-Python interoperability:\
\pard\tx940\tx1440\pardeftab720\li1440\fi-1440\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls92\ilvl1\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	
\f3 \uc0\u9702 
\f0 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Since virtually any operation on a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 PythonObject
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  can raise, the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 PythonObject
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  struct no longer implements the following traits: 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ImplicitlyBoolable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ImplicitlyIntable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
\pard\tx940\tx1440\pardeftab720\li1440\fi-1440\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls92\ilvl1
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	
\f3 \uc0\u9702 
\f0 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
PythonObject
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is no longer implicitly constructible from tuple or list literals. For example, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 var x : PythonObject = [1, 2, "foo"]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is no longer accepted. Instead, please use the new 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Python.list()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Python.tuple()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  factory methods. For example:\uc0\u8232 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  x \cf5 =\cf14  Python\cf5 .\cf15 list\cf5 (\cf18 1\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf18 2\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf19 "foo"\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\tx940\tx1440\pardeftab720\li1440\fi-1440\partightenfactor0
\ls92\ilvl1\cf10 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	
\f3 \uc0\u9702 
\f0 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\uc0\u8232 \cf14 \cb13 \uc0\u8232 
\fs32 \cf16 \cb1 \uc0\u8232 \cf17 \uc0\u8232 \cf16 \uc0\u8232 \cf14 \uc0\u8232 \cb13 \uc0\u8232 \cf2 \cb1 (The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 list()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 tuple()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  factory methods were originally added on 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 PythonObject
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , but have been moved to the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Python
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  struct.)\uc0\u8232 We hope to re-enable literal syntax in the future as the standard library matures.\
\pard\tx940\tx1440\pardeftab720\li1440\fi-1440\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls92\ilvl1
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	
\f3 \uc0\u9702 
\f0 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
PythonObject.from_borrowed_ptr()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  has been removed in favor of a constructor with a keyword-only 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 from_borrowed_ptr
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument.\
