The Mojo compiler now warns about 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 @parameter for
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  with large loop unrolling factor (>1024 by default), which can lead to long compilation time and large generated code size. Set 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 --loop-unrolling-warn-threshold
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to change default value to a different threshold or to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 0
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to disable the warning.\
\ls98\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The Mojo compile-time interpreter can now handle many more LLVM intrinsics, including ones that return floating point values. This allows functions like {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/math/math/round/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul round()}} to be constant folded when used in a compile-time context.\
\ls98\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The Mojo compiler now has only one compile-time interpreter. It had two previously: one to handle a few cases that were important for dependent types in the parser (but which also had many limitations), and the primary one that ran at "instantiation" time which is fully general. This was confusing and caused a wide range of bugs. We've now removed the special case parse-time interpreter, replacing it with a more general solution for dependent types. This change should be invisible to most users, but should resolve a number of long-standing bugs and significantly simplifies the compiler implementation, allowing us to move faster.\
