The set of automatically imported entities (types, aliases, functions) into users' Mojo programs has been dramatically reduced. Before, with the way the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 builtin
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  module was handled, all of the entities in the following modules would be automatically included:\uc0\u8232 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 memory
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 sys
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 os
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 utils
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 python
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 bit
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 random
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 math
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 builtin
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 collections
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 \uc0\u8232 Now, only the explicitly enumerated entities in 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 prelude/__init__.mojo
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  are the ones automatically imported into users' Mojo programs. This will break a lot of user code as users will need to explicitly import what they're using for cases previously commonly included before (such as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/collections/optional/Optional/"}}{\fldrslt 
