Mojo has changed how 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 def
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  function arguments are processed. Previously, by default, arguments to a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 def
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  were treated according to the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 owned
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  convention, which makes a copy of the value, enabling that value to be mutable in the callee.\uc0\u8232 This could lead to major performance issues because of the proliferation of unnecessary copies. It also required you to declare non-copyable types as 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 borrowed
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  explicitly. Now Mojo takes a different approach: 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 def
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  functions take arguments as 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 borrowed
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  by default (consistent with 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 fn
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  functions) but will make a local copy of the value only if the argument is mutated in the body of the function.\uc0\u8232 This improves consistency, performance, and ease of use.\
\ls129\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
