\fs48 \cf2 Tooling changes\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls143\ilvl0
\fs32 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The behavior of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 mojo build
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  when invoked without an output 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 -o
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument has changed slightly: 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 mojo build ./test-dir/program.mojo
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  now outputs an executable to the path 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ./program
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , whereas before it would output to the path 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ./test-dir/program
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
\ls143\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 mojo package
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  command no longer supports the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 -D
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  flag. All compilation environment flags should be provided at the point of package use (e.g. 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 mojo run
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  or 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 mojo build
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ).\
\ls143\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The REPL no longer allows type level variable declarations to be uninitialized, e.g. it will reject 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 var s: String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . This is because it does not do proper lifetime tracking (yet!) across cells, and so such code would lead to a crash. You can work around this by initializing to a dummy value and overwriting later. This limitation only applies to top level variables, variables in functions work as they always have.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

